  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok's in-feed standard template, a 720 by 1280 frame, shades 160 pixels at the top, 440 at the bottom and a column 120 pixels wide on the right from 560 pixels down, plus an 80-pixel strip down each side.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok's In-Feed Standard LTR template, downloaded from the auction in-feed spec page (June 2026) on October 8, 2026; dimension labels in the file, shaded areas checked pixel by pixel.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok's anchor templates, on a 540 by 960 frame, keep 126 pixels at the top and 60 at each side, then give the bottom 406, 439, 473 or 507 pixels as the caption grows from one line to four.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok's In-Feed with Anchor LTR templates, the same page, October 8, 2026; dimension labels printed in the four vertical files.</p>

  <p>The table scales both files to 1080 by 1920. That step is our arithmetic, a straight multiplication, not a TikTok figure. The standard file shades its side strips in a different color from the top, bottom and button column, and carries no legend, so we treat every shaded area as off limits for text and logos. In the anchor files, the anchor is drawn as a link card just above the username. The Arabic files mirror the layout, button column on the left.</p>

  <p>Put the two families together and one box clears every vertical template, whatever the caption length: 120 to 780 pixels across, 252 to 906 down. That box is derived from TikTok's files, not published by TikTok.</p>

  <p>TopView adds a second stage. The first 3 seconds play full screen, then the ad settles into the feed, and TikTok's page says the in-feed safe zone is the stricter of the two. It also says text left in the areas its preview tool marks red "should be rejected."</p>

  <p>There is no organic safe zone on any TikTok page. The ad templates are the only map TikTok itself draws, and they show the same furniture an ordinary post carries: the username, the caption, the sound line and the button column.</p>

  <h2>Why a TikTok upload fails, in TikTok's own words</h2>

  <p>TikTok's help pages list only a handful of causes. These are the ones it states.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The sound cut the clip.</strong> Pick a sound before you record or upload, and the video's length becomes the sound's length.</li>
    <li><strong>The web upload took the whole file.</strong> A browser upload sends the entire video. To trim it, TikTok says to upload in the app.</li>
    <li><strong>The app stalled.</strong> TikTok's fix list: switch between Wi-Fi and mobile data, clear the app's video cache, restart the app and the phone, and update to the latest version. A new post missing from your profile may be set to private.</li>
    <li><strong>The browser stalled.</strong> Refresh the page, then restart the browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Realistic AI content went out unlabeled.</strong> TikTok requires the label and may remove content without it. Files that carry Content Credentials can be labeled automatically, and that label can't be taken off.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The ads side is stricter. The review checklist, last updated February 2025, requires clear audio in video ads and rules out prompts for actions the app doesn't support, such as swiping up or a mouse cursor. Media has to meet the specs. The creative has to match the landing page. The reservation notes ask for video that isn't stretched or compressed, and TopView rules out QR codes, phone numbers and addresses.</p>

  <h2>How hubStudio publishes to TikTok (Beta)</h2>

  <p>TikTok publishing in the hubStudio app is in Beta. You connect your own TikTok account in My Connections, and nobody else on the team can post with it. A post carries one MP4, MOV or WEBM clip of 3 seconds to 10 minutes. Some accounts are allowed less, and the publishing step says so before you send. That window is shorter than TikTok's 60-minute upload, so a longer cut goes up through TikTok's own uploader.</p>

  <p>The clip can be rendered with AI, picked from the Assets Library or uploaded. The Video editor applies the 9:16 frame and shows in red where TikTok's caption, sound line and buttons cover the picture. The caption editor holds up to 2,200 characters. On the publishing step you set who can see the post, whether people can comment, duet or stitch, any brand disclosure, and the AI-generated label, which comes already ticked for a clip rendered with AI in hubStudio.</p>

  <p>Then publish now or schedule it. The queue is checked every five minutes. A temporary problem on TikTok's side is retried, three attempts in all. A content problem isn't, and the person who queued the post gets an email with what TikTok said. No connected account? Publish manually: TikTok's upload page opens with the caption on your clipboard.</p>

  <p>Publishing costs nothing. A render is charged against your prepaid balance, at the price shown before you run it.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/app/publish">publishing page of the app</a> lists the other networks. If you'd rather the studio made the videos, start with the <a href="/services/design/short-video">short video design service</a>; the <a href="/solutions/platforms/tiktok">TikTok platform page</a> sets out both routes.</p>

  <h2>Douyin is a different spec sheet</h2>

  <p>Douyin, the Chinese app from the same parent company, is a separate product with separate specs. Don't build a Douyin cut to the numbers on this page, and don't trust a Douyin guide that quotes them. <a href="/resources/insights/douyin-video-specs-safe-zones">Douyin video specs and safe zones</a> covers the organic frame, and <a href="/resources/insights/douyin-ad-creative-specs-by-format">Douyin ad creative specs by format</a> covers paid placements. Both sit with this page on the <a href="/resources/specs">platform specs hub</a>.</p>

